{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01DXfAkwgjYHiY17vpwuLbUq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tfslib\n2\t=====\n3\t\n4\tFinite state transducer library. Minimalist pure C implementation. This project is mostly inspired by OpenFST library (http://www.openfst.org) by Michael Riley and Mehriar Mohri. The main focus here is performance and simplicity. \n5\t\n6\t<img src=\"examples/lev.png\" height=\"250\" width=\"500\">\n7\t\n8\tSetup\n9\t-----\n10\t\n11\tC99 compiler is required. Just run:\n12\t\n13\t```bash\n14\tgit clone https://github.com/c0stya/fslib.git\n15\tcd fslib && make\n16\t```\n17\t\n18\tTo run compile and run tests:\n19\t\n20\t```bash\n21\tmake tests\n22\t```\n23\t\n24\tUsage\n25\t-----\n26\t\n27\t### Compilation\n28\t\n29\tLet's create an automaton that accepts \"(01)\\*\" regular language. We need a *fscompile* command:\n30\t\n31\t```bash\n32\techo  \"\\\n33\t0 1 0 0\n34\t1 0 1 1\n35\t0\" | fscompile > automaton.t\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\tEach line contains either transition or final state.\n39\t\n40\tTransition line format:\n41\t* source state\n42\t* destination state\n43\t* input label\n44\t* output label\n45\t* (optional) weight\n46\t\n47\tFinal state line contains only a number of a state.\n48\t\n49\t### Printing\n50\t\n51\tTo print it just invoke *fsprint* command:\n52\t```bash\n53\tfsprint automaton.t\n54\t```\n55\tor as usual pipe:\n56\t```bash\n57\tcat automaton.t | fsprint\n58\t```\n59\t\n60\t### Composition\n61\t\n62\tTo compose transducers one should use the *fscompose* command:\n63\t\n64\t```bash\n65\tfscompose aut0.t aut1.t > aut3.t\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tIt could be done in the pipe style with more then two automata:\n69\t```bash\n70\tcat aut0.t | fscompose - aut1.t | fscompose - aut2.t > aut3.t\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\tWarning: a proper sorting of input/output arcs could be significantly increase the performance.\n74\t\n75\t### Shortest path\n76\t\n77\tUse *fsshort* command to get the a transducer with the shortest path:\n78\t\n79\t```bash\n80\tfsshort automaton.t > shortest_path.t\n81\t```\n82\t\n83\t### Drawing\n84\t\n85\tWe could draw automaton using *fsdraw* command. To actually render it you need *dot* utility from the Graphviz package.  Make sure you have Graphviz (http://www.graphviz.org) package properly installed.\n86\t\n87\tTo render a transducer to a png file use the following:\n88\t\n89\t```bash\n90\tfsdraw automaton.t | dot -Tpng > automaton.png\n91\t```\n92\t\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e88f162a-08e0-465a-8324-81480e433fd1","uuid":"af7add19-d4ee-40ae-80e6-619c390df780","timestamp":"2026-05-29T01:33:32.855Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/harvest-crust-4xUKOi/project/c_src/README.md","content":"fslib\n=====\n\nFinite state transducer library. Minimalist pure C implementation. This project is mostly inspired by OpenFST library (http://www.openfst.org) by Michael Riley and Mehriar Mohri. The main focus here is performance and simplicity. \n\n<img src=\"examples/lev.png\" height=\"250\" width=\"500\">\n\nSetup\n-----\n\nC99 compiler is required. Just run:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/c0stya/fslib.git\ncd fslib && make\n```\n\nTo run compile and run tests:\n\n```bash\nmake tests\n```\n\nUsage\n-----\n\n### Compilation\n\nLet's create an automaton that accepts \"(01)\\*\" regular language. We need a *fscompile* command:\n\n```bash\necho  \"\\\n0 1 0 0\n1 0 1 1\n0\" | fscompile > automaton.t\n```\n\nEach line contains either transition or final state.\n\nTransition line format:\n* source state\n* destination state\n* input label\n* output label\n* (optional) weight\n\nFinal state line contains only a number of a state.\n\n### Printing\n\nTo print it just invoke *fsprint* command:\n```bash\nfsprint automaton.t\n```\nor as usual pipe:\n```bash\ncat automaton.t | fsprint\n```\n\n### Composition\n\nTo compose transducers one should use the *fscompose* command:\n\n```bash\nfscompose aut0.t aut1.t > aut3.t\n```\n\nIt could be done in the pipe style with more then two automata:\n```bash\ncat aut0.t | fscompose - aut1.t | fscompose - aut2.t > aut3.t\n```\n\nWarning: a proper sorting of input/output arcs could be significantly increase the performance.\n\n### Shortest path\n\nUse *fsshort* command to get the a transducer with the shortest path:\n\n```bash\nfsshort automaton.t > shortest_path.t\n```\n\n### Drawing\n\nWe could draw automaton using *fsdraw* command. To actually render it you need *dot* utility from the Graphviz package.  Make sure you have Graphviz (http://www.graphviz.org) package properly installed.\n\nTo render a transducer to a png file use the following:\n\n```bash\nfsdraw automaton.t | dot -Tpng > automaton.png\n```\n\n","numLines":93,"startLine":1,"totalLines":93}}}
